A||N||7||Lunapark||Corvette Brakes to the Rescue||||10||
Z||000000||Lunapark||09-11-2000||10:18 PM||||I wanted to share my experience with the group. For those that would upgrade their brakes, Here is a great solution that has worked for me.<P>I had a shop do some work on my 87 in regards to a convert from a 2.8 to a 3.4. While they had it I had them install a set of 95 Corvette rotors and Calipers on the front and keep the 88 Rotors and Calipers on the rear. The rear was 88 because the whole rear end was upgraded to 88. These are parts that I bought from the local GM Shop for under $500.00. I spent $50 per rotors to have them redrilled to fit the Fiero. <P>This setup will stop the car from 100mph to 0 in under 115 feet. It is the best setup I have experienced yet. I have the Wilwood setup on another car of mine and I like this better. The best part about it is that I can go downt the street to get pads and calipers if needed and any shop can change the brakes if they can change a Corvette brake. <P>Just thought I would share.<BR><P>------------------<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000001||lou_dias||09-11-2000||10:42 PM||||Too bad you didn't take pictures...<BR>Did you need bigger wheels?||||reg||1
Z||000002||mshill||09-11-2000||11:54 PM||||Are there any other modifications required besides drilling the hubs for the Fiero bolt pattern?  Like Lou asked, what is the minimum wheel size?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Lunapark||09-12-2000||07:10 AM||||I am using 17" wheels that I bought at Tire Rack for no more that $100.00 each. Go Figure. I will take some pictures. As I don't have a digital Camera It will take a couple of days. The kit was bought from RCC Speciality. At the time that I bought the kit he was selling the rear setup but it didn't work with the Emergency brake. He has since then solved this problem. I decided to go without the rear Corvette after driving the car and realizing that they were not needed. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||lawrence||09-12-2000||12:34 PM||||Good thing you don't have an air bag in that car or it would deploy!<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||09-12-2000||09:24 PM||||Vette brakes for under $500? sounds pretty good! I've got 11.? brakes on the rear of my Rossa replica project and need to upgrade the front as well. I'm using 17" wheels, so that shouldn't be a problem. Thanks for the info, but I too would love to see some pictures. What was included with the RCC kit? Did you have to upgrade the master cylinder?<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Lunapark||09-12-2000||10:29 PM||||I used steel brake lines, Master Cylinder stayed the same. I bought the parts my self and the kit stuff from RCC because I have a resellers license and I purchase parts from the dealership at cost. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||BBBAD GT||09-12-2000||10:40 PM||||I have the RCC 11" brake set up on all fours on the V8 Fiero, and I must say they stop pretty darn well.  They also carried with it a $1,300 price tag.  The min wheel you need is a 16" (I'm running 17").  <P>Lunapark:  what space saver are you using, if at all?  I know the stock 15" space saver don't fit.  Right now, I'm using a couple cans of 'fix a flat'   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">     <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A><p>[This message has been edited by BBBAD GT (edited 09-12-2000).]||||reg||1
